  "content": {
    "id": "p35-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "During the autumn, the rate of decrease of the fly population $F$ is proportional to the excess over $30\\,000$, that is, $\\dfrac{dF}{dt}=-k(F-30\\,000)$, for some positive constant $k$. Initially there are $1\\,000\\,000$ flies, and ten days later the number has halved. Let $t$ be the time in days.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p35-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $F=30\\,000+Be^{-kt}$ satisfies the differential equation, where $B$ is constant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{dF}{dt}=-Bke^{-kt}=-k(F-30\\,000)$, so the differential equation is satisfied.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}F&=30\\,000+Be^{-kt}\\\\\\frac{dF}{dt}&=-Bke^{-kt}.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso,\n$$\\begin{align*}-k(F-30\\,000)&=-k(30\\,000+Be^{-kt}-30\\,000)\\\\&=-k(Be^{-kt})\\\\&=-Bke^{-kt}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\dfrac{dF}{dt}=-k(F-30\\,000)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the values of $B$ and $k$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$B=970\\,000$, $k=\\dfrac{1}{10}\\ln\\!\\left(\\dfrac{97}{47}\\right)$.",
          "worked": "At $t=0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}1\\,000\\,000&=30\\,000+B\\\\B&=970\\,000.\\end{align*}$$\nAt $t=10$,\n$$\\begin{align*}500\\,000&=30\\,000+970\\,000e^{-10k}\\\\470\\,000&=970\\,000e^{-10k}\\\\e^{-10k}&=\\frac{47}{97}\\\\-10k&=\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{47}{97}\\right)\\\\k&=\\frac{1}{10}\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{97}{47}\\right).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the population after four weeks, correct to the nearest $1000$ flies.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$158\\,000$ flies.",
          "worked": "Four weeks is $28$ days. Using $k=\\dfrac{1}{10}\\ln\\!\\left(\\dfrac{97}{47}\\right)$,\n$$\\begin{align*}F(28)&=30\\,000+970\\,000e^{-28k}\\\\&=30\\,000+970\\,000\\left(\\frac{47}{97}\\right)^{14/5}\\\\&\\approx158\\,000\\text{ flies}\\quad\\text{(nearest 1000).}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q4-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find when the population reaches $35\\,000$, correct to the nearest day.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$73$ days.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}35\\,000&=30\\,000+970\\,000e^{-kt}\\\\5000&=970\\,000e^{-kt}\\\\e^{-kt}&=\\frac{1}{194}\\\\-kt&=\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{194}\\right)\\\\t&=\\frac{\\ln(1/194)}{-k}\\\\&=\\frac{10\\ln 194}{\\ln(97/47)}\\\\&\\approx73\\text{ days}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
  },
